Kioptrix Level 1 — Penetration Testing Report

Target: Kioptrix Level 1 — VulnHub Lab
Assessment Type: Internal Network Penetration Test
Attacker Machine: Kali Linux
Target IP: 192.168.1.105
Objective: Identify exposed services, determine vulnerable software, obtain unauthorized access, and demonstrate the maximum achievable impact within the controlled lab environment.

1. Executive Summary

This assessment was performed against the intentionally vulnerable Kioptrix Level 1 virtual machine in an isolated lab environment.

The assessment began with network discovery followed by a full TCP port and service scan. Several exposed services were identified, including SSH, HTTP, SMB, HTTPS, CUPS, MySQL, and RPC.

Further enumeration of the SMB service identified Samba 2.2.1a, an obsolete version affected by the trans2open buffer-overflow vulnerability (CVE-2003-0201).

The vulnerability could be exploited remotely without authentication and resulted in command execution with root privileges.

The final attack chain was:

Network Discovery → Full Port Scan → SMB Enumeration → Samba Version Identification → Vulnerability Research → Remote Exploitation → Root Shell

Unlike some assessments where initial access must be followed by local privilege escalation, this vulnerability provided root-level access immediately.

2. Reconnaissance
2.1 Network Discovery

Because the target was running as a DHCP-assigned virtual machine, I first performed local network discovery using netdiscover.

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/kioptrix]
└─$ sudo netdiscover -r 192.168.1.0/24

