11. Key Findings
Finding 1 — Critically Outdated Samba Service

Severity: Critical

The target exposed Samba 2.2.1a through port 139.

This version is affected by the historical trans2open vulnerability, which can allow unauthenticated remote code execution.

Impact:

Remote system compromise
Arbitrary command execution
Root-level access
Complete loss of system confidentiality, integrity, and availability

Recommendation:

Upgrade Samba to a supported and patched version.
Remove obsolete SMB implementations.
Restrict SMB exposure through network segmentation and firewall rules.
Disable unnecessary SMB services.
Monitor externally reachable legacy services.
Finding 2 — Legacy Operating System / Software Stack

Severity: Critical

Multiple extremely outdated services were exposed, including:

Apache 1.3.20
OpenSSH 2.9p2
OpenSSL 0.9.6b
CUPS 1.1
Samba 2.2.1a

The age of these components significantly increased the likelihood of publicly known vulnerabilities being exploitable.

Recommendation:

Replace unsupported operating systems.
Upgrade all exposed network services.
Establish a regular patch-management process.
Remove obsolete services that are no longer required.
Finding 3 — Excessive Network Exposure

Severity: High

The host exposed numerous services across different protocols:

SSH
HTTP
HTTPS
RPC
SMB
IPP
MySQL

Exposing multiple legacy services increases the available attack surface.

Recommendation:

Apply the principle of least exposure:

Only expose services that are operationally required.
Restrict administrative services to trusted networks.
Use host-based and network firewalls.
Segment sensitive services.
Regularly perform external and internal attack-surface reviews.
Finding 4 — Legacy HTTP/SSL Configuration

Severity: High

The HTTP service disclosed:

Apache/1.3.20
mod_ssl/2.8.4
OpenSSL/0.9.6b

These components are severely outdated.

Nikto also identified the HTTP TRACE method as enabled.

Recommendation:

Upgrade Apache and OpenSSL.
Disable unnecessary HTTP methods such as TRACE.
Use modern TLS configurations.
Remove obsolete cryptographic protocols and libraries.
